        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">COPD exacerbation, treated with bronchodilators and steroids</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, thank you for referring your 66-year-old male patient, who was admitted on 31/05/2014 with an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Chest X-ray showed pulmonary hyperinflation without pneumonic infiltrates. Blood gas analysis confirmed hypoxaemia with mild hypercapnia. Blood tests revealed mild inflammatory markers. He was treated with inhaled bronchodilators via nebuliser, a course of systemic corticosteroids, and supplemental oxygen therapy. Vital signs were monitored continuously throughout the stay. The patient responded well to treatment with significant symptomatic improvement. Discharge education regarding inhaler technique and exacerbation management was provided. We recommend continued follow-up and optimisation of his maintenance inhaler therapy. Please do not hesitate to contact us for further information.</div>"
            },
